237707 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ccb96764-d461-5bc7-bf26-9228be058bcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237858 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e84c628-87b9-5d74-b46c-f0558ebf65c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237858 #edb/ref 1074 "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substitute this into the second equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(3)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1+3=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(2)+(3)=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1-3+2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$,  ${x}_{2}=2$, and ${x}_{3}=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,2,3⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237931 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+2y=1 \\\\ 2x-3y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25d74c84-9d1d-5f1c-8223-f549d1370a13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237931 #edb/ref 1074 "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237987 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nI. The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $\n\nII. The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n$\n\nIII. The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2215b590-8140-5323-92bf-8ae24e1b7bb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237987 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/73/732955302ca49a41f6c43cf242d7c869e630e88d804c4b56a5beb3b081f2d230.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined everywhere, except where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238005 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238005 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fb/fb61c048eb7eac87f82c53717f8f67251b59610817ddf1f267e80543c0c86959.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-1$\n\nII. $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=-1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f635edc1-2f85-538f-89d8-4edf62a22ccc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238005 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=-1,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a critical point at $x=-1$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=-1$ since $f(-1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238063 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238063 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238063 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238063 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\nI. The function has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.$\n\nII. The domain of the function is $[-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nIII. The period of the function is $\\pi .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e25815e7-4d17-5965-b1f7-fc15c12bd9d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238063 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/77/7785ad73f6e5212772c3053780ffd055f37cfb39927b6ddc3e40cd3a55b8ba4e.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The vertical asymptotes of the function are integer multiples of $\\pi $, i.e., $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The domain of the function is all real numbers except the values where the asymptotes occur, i.e., $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238093 #edb/ref 1080 "x+1=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238093 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238093 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01ae9f63-6af5-5a84-a916-c7136c71812f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238119 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238119 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238119 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0484eaf7-9412-5914-9529-5a91fc451c8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238166 #edb/ref 1080 "x+2=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=-6t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238166 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238166 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c416e9a-7d8a-5552-8632-d76acfb50f11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238184 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238184 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238184 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f36c6d0-d4a6-5f4b-8a3b-e70b9d746666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238203 #edb/ref 1080 "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238203 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238203 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11b040a2-7645-58f8-9b41-03e61af92932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238225 #edb/ref 1080 "x+2=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-3=-4t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238225 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238225 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "146d60fa-eb3e-519e-a6dc-bd88e4c1fb74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238